r maybe 1.2 voices  in the poll rather than one if there are  too many college educated men  those people might count for 0.9 voices  in the poll  instead of one voice in the poll and in  that way  pollsters take their sample and adjust  adjusted to reflect  what they think the world really looks  like in 2016  many pollsters were slow to catch on to  key shifts in the electorate  like the degree to which educational  attainment was becoming a determining  factor in how someone might vote  that led pollsters in battleground  states to draw samples that did not  match the true makeup of the voter pool  the fact that america has become so much  more polarized  among gender lines and along the lines  of education  has consequences for polling uh two  groups in particular  are worth looking at one is white men  who don't have a college degree often  called white working class  men and then let's consider white women  with a college  degree these two groups have always been  different in their political  preferences as far back as our polling  goes but the differences were not that  great  but then in about 2010 these two groups  exploded apart and really their  preferences  on who they wanted to vote for really  changed when you have  people separated that much you  need to be sure that you're measuring  education accurately  kennedy says that in this election cycle  the three challenges from 2016 are still  in play  but to a lesser degree undecided voters  breaking  um you know in a strong numbers for one  candidate late  that could happen this year but what we  do know what's very clear  is that there's just fewer people in  that bucket and in terms of that  technical issue  of waiting by education the good news is  that  many state pollsters who had not been  doing that have fixed  their methods since 2016 and added  a waiting adjustment for education to  their polls so that's good  but not everybody has some posters still  refuse to do that and some posters  unfortu